use /lib/UiComponent

from /behaviors/Traversal import Traversal
from /components/layout/Grid import Grid
from /components/overlays/Popover import Popover

export ui-component DatePicker:
    description:
        A date picker holds a date, or a range of dates, as its value. The user
        can type the date, or pick it from a calendar that pops up. Dates are
        shown and read in the user's local format.

    composes:
        - @{Popover}
        - @{Grid}

    behaviors:
        - @{Traversal}

    value:
        A plain calendar date, such as 25 September 2026, with no time of day
        and no time zone.

    typing:
        The user can type a date in their local format. It's read when they
        finish. If it can't be read as a date, an error is shown and the value
        doesn't change.

    calendar:
        A button next to the field opens a calendar showing one month. It
        opens on the month of the current value, or today's month if there is
        none, with that day focused. Today is marked. The week starts on the
        day that's usual for the user's locale.

    calendarKeyboard:
        The arrow keys move a day at a time left and right, and a week at a
        time up and down, moving into the next or previous month as needed.
        Home and End go to the start and end of the week. Page Up and Page
        Down move a month, and with Shift, a year. Enter picks the focused day.

    picking:
        Picking a day sets the value and closes the calendar, and focus goes
        back to the field. Escape closes it without changing anything.

    range:
        For a range, the user picks two days, and the days between them are
        highlighted as they move. The earlier day becomes the start whichever
        order they were picked in. The calendar closes after the second pick.

    limits:
        The component can set an earliest and latest date, and mark other days
        as unavailable, such as weekends. Unavailable days are shown but can't
        be picked.

    nonGoals:
        - No time of day. That's a TimePicker; a date and time together is the
            two combined
        - No preset ranges like "Last 7 days". The app can add those
        - Which calendar systems are supported, beyond the usual one, is up to
            the chosen library
